{"message": [{"role": "user", "content": "다음 코드를 리뷰해줘:\n\n```\n@@ -1370,6 +1392,32 @@ def url_defaults(self, f):\n         self.url_default_functions.setdefault(None, []).append(f)\n         return f\n \n+    def _find_error_handler(self, e):\n+        \"\"\"Finds a registered error handler for the request’s blueprint.\n+        If neither blueprint nor App has a suitable handler registered, returns None\n+        \"\"\"\n+        exc_class, code = self._ensure_exc_class(e)\n+        \n+        def find_superclass(d):\n+            if not d:\n+                return None\n+            for superclass in exc_class.mro():\n+                if superclass is BaseException:\n+                    return None\n+                handler = d.get(superclass)\n+                if handler is not None:\n+                    return handler\n```"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "you mean `d[exc_class]`. `e` is an exception instance.\n\nand that would create an edge case:\n\n`````` python\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n# hierarchy: Exception → OSError → ConnectionError → ConnectionAbortedError\n\n@app.errorhandler(OSError)\ndef handle_os_error(e):\n    ...\n\napp.run()\n\nsomewhere during runtime some connection aborts, a ConnectionAbortedError instance is raised. now, due to your caching: `d[ConnectionAbortedError] is d[OSError]`\n\nalso during runtime (after that), some new error handler is registered. (not inconceivable):\n\n```python\n@app.errorhandler(ConnectionError)\ndef handle_connection_error(e):\n    ...\n``````\n\nwell fuck, now another ConnectionAbortedError instance being raised won’t trigger the newly registered error handler but the cached one!\n\n---\n\nwe can circumvent this by instead having a separate dict holding all cached handlers, and clearing all superclasses of a newly registered exception from that cache handler dict. like so:\n\n``` python\n# in __init__:\nself._error_handler_cache = {}\n\n# in _register_error_handler:\nfor superclass in exc_class.mro():\n    try:\n        del self._error_handler_cache[superclass]\n    except KeyError:\n        pass\n\n# in _find_error_handler → find_superclass (after “if not d: return None”):\nhandler = self._error_handler_cache.get(exc_class)\nif handler is not None:\n    return handler\n\n# in _find_error_handler → find_superclass (before returning the handler):\nself._error_handler_cache[exc_class] = handler\n```"}]}
{"message": [{"role": "user", "content": "다음 코드를 리뷰해줘:\n\n```\n@@ -1370,6 +1392,32 @@ def url_defaults(self, f):\n         self.url_default_functions.setdefault(None, []).append(f)\n         return f\n \n+    def _find_error_handler(self, e):\n+        \"\"\"Finds a registered error handler for the request’s blueprint.\n+        If neither blueprint nor App has a suitable handler registered, returns None\n+        \"\"\"\n+        exc_class, code = self._ensure_exc_class(e)\n+        \n+        def find_superclass(d):\n+            if not d:\n+                return None\n+            for superclass in exc_class.mro():\n+                if superclass is BaseException:\n+                    return None\n+                handler = d.get(superclass)\n+                if handler is not None:\n+                    return handler\n```"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "> You mean exc_class\n\nAh, yes, sorry.\n\n> also during runtime (after that), some new error handler is registered. (not inconceivable)\n\nThis is explicitly forbidden by Flask. See the `setupmethod` decorator. So basically you don't have any requests until all your errorhandlers are set up."}]}
{"message": [{"role": "user", "content": "다음 코드를 리뷰해줘:\n\n```\n@@ -1133,16 +1150,21 @@ def register_error_handler(self, code_or_exception, f):\n \n     @setupmethod\n     def _register_error_handler(self, key, code_or_exception, f):\n-        if isinstance(code_or_exception, HTTPException):\n-            code_or_exception = code_or_exception.code\n-        if isinstance(code_or_exception, integer_types):\n-            assert code_or_exception != 500 or key is None, \\\n-                'It is currently not possible to register a 500 internal ' \\\n-                'server error on a per-blueprint level.'\n-            self.error_handler_spec.setdefault(key, {})[code_or_exception] = f\n-        else:\n-            self.error_handler_spec.setdefault(key, {}).setdefault(None, []) \\\n-                .append((code_or_exception, f))\n+        \"\"\"\n+        :type key: None|str\n+        :type code_or_exception: int|T<=Exception\n+        :type f: callable\n+        \"\"\"\n+        if isinstance(code_or_exception, HTTPException):  # old broken behavior\n+            raise Value\n... (생략)\n```"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "i thought about that as well and thought that it’s clearer this way. all handlers are neatly grouped and accessing them in debug mode using `self.error_handler_spec[None][403]` will list you everything.\n\nbut i’m not particularly attached to this.\n\nPS: we can preserve partial backwards compat by using a dict subclass for the `error_handler_spec[foobar]` level by redefining `__setitem__`:\n\n``` python\nclass BackwardsCompatDict(dict):\n    def __init__(self, app):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.app = app\n    def __setitem__(self, key, value):\n        if callable(value) and not isinstance(value, dict):\n            exc_class, code = self.app._ensure_exc_class(key)\n            self.setdefault(code, {})[exc_class] = value\n        else:\n            super().__setitem__(key, handler_or_handlerdict)\n\napp.error_handler_spec[None][403] = forbidden_handler\nassert app.error_handler_spec[None][403][Forbidden] is forbidden_handler\n```"}]}
