{"message": [{"role": "user", "content": "다음 코드를 리뷰해줘:\n\n```\n@@ -1370,6 +1390,33 @@ def url_defaults(self, f):\n         self.url_default_functions.setdefault(None, []).append(f)\n         return f\n \n+    def _find_error_handler(self, e):\n+        \"\"\"Finds a registered error handler for the request’s blueprint.\n+        If neither blueprint nor App has a suitable handler registered, returns None\n+        \"\"\"\n+        exc_class, code = self._get_exc_class_and_code(type(e))\n+        \n+        def find_superclass(d):\n+            if not d:\n+                return None\n+            for superclass in exc_class.mro():\n+                if superclass is BaseException:\n+                    return None\n+                handler = d.get(superclass)\n+                if handler is not None:\n+                    d[exc_class] = handler  # cache for next time exc_class is raised\n```"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "This might be confusing during debugging, since the handler dict mutates during execution, but you might argue that when debugging you should be aware of the cache, so I don't feel strongly about this, I just don't like premature optimization."}]}
{"message": [{"role": "user", "content": "다음 코드를 리뷰해줘:\n\n```\n@@ -1370,6 +1390,33 @@ def url_defaults(self, f):\n         self.url_default_functions.setdefault(None, []).append(f)\n         return f\n \n+    def _find_error_handler(self, e):\n+        \"\"\"Finds a registered error handler for the request’s blueprint.\n+        If neither blueprint nor App has a suitable handler registered, returns None\n+        \"\"\"\n+        exc_class, code = self._get_exc_class_and_code(type(e))\n+        \n+        def find_superclass(d):\n+            if not d:\n+                return None\n+            for superclass in exc_class.mro():\n+                if superclass is BaseException:\n+                    return None\n+                handler = d.get(superclass)\n+                if handler is not None:\n+                    d[exc_class] = handler  # cache for next time exc_class is raised\n```"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Good point about debugging, with that in mind I'd like to remove that cache as well. @adambyrtek care to setup a PR, since this is already merged?"}]}
{"message": [{"role": "user", "content": "다음 코드를 리뷰해줘:\n\n```\n@@ -1370,6 +1390,33 @@ def url_defaults(self, f):\n         self.url_default_functions.setdefault(None, []).append(f)\n         return f\n \n+    def _find_error_handler(self, e):\n+        \"\"\"Finds a registered error handler for the request’s blueprint.\n+        If neither blueprint nor App has a suitable handler registered, returns None\n+        \"\"\"\n+        exc_class, code = self._get_exc_class_and_code(type(e))\n+        \n+        def find_superclass(d):\n+            if not d:\n+                return None\n+            for superclass in exc_class.mro():\n+                if superclass is BaseException:\n+                    return None\n+                handler = d.get(superclass)\n+                if handler is not None:\n+                    d[exc_class] = handler  # cache for next time exc_class is raised\n```"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "i’m curious: what’s the problem during debugging?"}]}
{"message": [{"role": "user", "content": "다음 코드를 리뷰해줘:\n\n```\n@@ -1370,6 +1390,33 @@ def url_defaults(self, f):\n         self.url_default_functions.setdefault(None, []).append(f)\n         return f\n \n+    def _find_error_handler(self, e):\n+        \"\"\"Finds a registered error handler for the request’s blueprint.\n+        If neither blueprint nor App has a suitable handler registered, returns None\n+        \"\"\"\n+        exc_class, code = self._get_exc_class_and_code(type(e))\n+        \n+        def find_superclass(d):\n+            if not d:\n+                return None\n+            for superclass in exc_class.mro():\n+                if superclass is BaseException:\n+                    return None\n+                handler = d.get(superclass)\n+                if handler is not None:\n+                    d[exc_class] = handler  # cache for next time exc_class is raised\n```"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "They might not invalidate themselves correctly when the programmer is testing a very recent change they made, or otherwise warp results. As @adambyrtek said, you could argue that the programmer should simply be _aware_ of those caches (as is the case with e.g. browser caches -- I always use icognito mode to test e.g. redirecting behavior), but in our case, it's questionable whether this cache even gives us any performance benefits (which is clearly the case with browser caches).\n\nAlso: In my opinion, caches are a tempting (and usually bad) answer to CPU-bound performance problems: They are simply there for delaying the writing of a more performant algorithm."}]}
{"message": [{"role": "user", "content": "다음 코드를 리뷰해줘:\n\n```\n@@ -1370,6 +1390,33 @@ def url_defaults(self, f):\n         self.url_default_functions.setdefault(None, []).append(f)\n         return f\n \n+    def _find_error_handler(self, e):\n+        \"\"\"Finds a registered error handler for the request’s blueprint.\n+        If neither blueprint nor App has a suitable handler registered, returns None\n+        \"\"\"\n+        exc_class, code = self._get_exc_class_and_code(type(e))\n+        \n+        def find_superclass(d):\n+            if not d:\n+                return None\n+            for superclass in exc_class.mro():\n+                if superclass is BaseException:\n+                    return None\n+                handler = d.get(superclass)\n+                if handler is not None:\n+                    d[exc_class] = handler  # cache for next time exc_class is raised\n```"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "hmm, as i said: it’s forbidden to register new routes after app start, so the cache will never be invalidated.\n\nthat’s the main reason i implemented it: very simple and no potential for failure.\n\nand the more efficient algorithm may be to statically include all exceptions in the mapping upon app start. so basically replacing my JIT cache with an AOT cache :smile:"}]}
{"message": [{"role": "user", "content": "다음 코드를 리뷰해줘:\n\n```\n@@ -1370,6 +1390,33 @@ def url_defaults(self, f):\n         self.url_default_functions.setdefault(None, []).append(f)\n         return f\n \n+    def _find_error_handler(self, e):\n+        \"\"\"Finds a registered error handler for the request’s blueprint.\n+        If neither blueprint nor App has a suitable handler registered, returns None\n+        \"\"\"\n+        exc_class, code = self._get_exc_class_and_code(type(e))\n+        \n+        def find_superclass(d):\n+            if not d:\n+                return None\n+            for superclass in exc_class.mro():\n+                if superclass is BaseException:\n+                    return None\n+                handler = d.get(superclass)\n+                if handler is not None:\n+                    d[exc_class] = handler  # cache for next time exc_class is raised\n```"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "> and the more efficient algorithm may be to statically include all exceptions in the mapping upon app start. so basically replacing my JIT cache with an AOT cache :smile:\n\nThat's impossible, we can't reach subclasses from parent classes.\n\nHonestly I think instead of proving that additional code doesn't cause problems, it should be proven that the absence of the code doesn't cause problems. IOW, the right for the cache to exist doesn't lie in \"it doesn't cause any problems\", but rather \"it improves performance\". And that has to be proven instead of saying \"why not\"."}]}
