{"message": [{"role": "user", "content": "다음 코드를 리뷰해줘:\n\n```\n@@ -176,16 +176,24 @@ the `template_folder` parameter to the :class:`Blueprint` constructor::\n \n     admin = Blueprint('admin', __name__, template_folder='templates')\n \n-As for static files, the path can be absolute or relative to the blueprint\n-resource folder.  The template folder is added to the searchpath of\n-templates but with a lower priority than the actual application's template\n-folder.  That way you can easily override templates that a blueprint\n-provides in the actual application.\n+For static files, the path can be absolute or relative to the blueprint\n+resource folder.  \n+\n+The template folder is added to the search path of templates but with a lower \n+priority than the actual application's template folder. That way you can \n+easily override templates that a blueprint provides in the actual application. \n+This also means that you'll have to make sure that no blueprint template shares \n+the same relative path with any other blueprint or actual application template \n+else a n\n... (생략)\n```"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The way this is currently worded might imply:\n1. the \"name conflict\" will throw an exception\n2. You must always make sure the names are unique, when in fact you might (rarely) wish to leverage this behavior. \n\nMaybe instead say something like:\n\n> This also means that if you don't want a blueprint template to be accidentally overridden, make sure that no other blueprint or actual application template has the same relative path."}]}
{"message": [{"role": "user", "content": "다음 코드를 리뷰해줘:\n\n```\n@@ -176,16 +176,24 @@ the `template_folder` parameter to the :class:`Blueprint` constructor::\n \n     admin = Blueprint('admin', __name__, template_folder='templates')\n \n-As for static files, the path can be absolute or relative to the blueprint\n-resource folder.  The template folder is added to the searchpath of\n-templates but with a lower priority than the actual application's template\n-folder.  That way you can easily override templates that a blueprint\n-provides in the actual application.\n+For static files, the path can be absolute or relative to the blueprint\n+resource folder.  \n+\n+The template folder is added to the search path of templates but with a lower \n+priority than the actual application's template folder. That way you can \n```"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "What about adding an additional sentence here explaining how the priority order is resolved when two blueprint templates conflict?"}]}
{"message": [{"role": "user", "content": "다음 코드를 리뷰해줘:\n\n```\n@@ -176,16 +176,24 @@ the `template_folder` parameter to the :class:`Blueprint` constructor::\n \n     admin = Blueprint('admin', __name__, template_folder='templates')\n \n-As for static files, the path can be absolute or relative to the blueprint\n-resource folder.  The template folder is added to the searchpath of\n-templates but with a lower priority than the actual application's template\n-folder.  That way you can easily override templates that a blueprint\n-provides in the actual application.\n+For static files, the path can be absolute or relative to the blueprint\n+resource folder.  \n+\n+The template folder is added to the search path of templates but with a lower \n+priority than the actual application's template folder. That way you can \n```"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "In  [flask.templating.DispatchingJinjaLoader#_iter_loaders](https://github.com/pallets/flask/blob/master/flask/templating.py#L87) looks like the first loader checked is the main app loader and then it walks the blueprint loaders by the order they were registered\n\nAlso [this old answer](http://stackoverflow.com/a/8737529/1631135) suggests the same.\n\n> First it checks if the app's global loader, which is app.jinja_loader can locate the file. Failing that, it checks all application blueprints (in order of registration, AFAIK) for blueprint.jinja_loader in an attempt to locate the file. \n\nHow does this sound?\n\n> When multiple blueprints provide the same relative template path the first blueprint registered takes precedence over the others."}]}
{"message": [{"role": "user", "content": "다음 코드를 리뷰해줘:\n\n```\n@@ -176,16 +176,24 @@ the `template_folder` parameter to the :class:`Blueprint` constructor::\n \n     admin = Blueprint('admin', __name__, template_folder='templates')\n \n-As for static files, the path can be absolute or relative to the blueprint\n-resource folder.  The template folder is added to the searchpath of\n-templates but with a lower priority than the actual application's template\n-folder.  That way you can easily override templates that a blueprint\n-provides in the actual application.\n+For static files, the path can be absolute or relative to the blueprint\n+resource folder.  \n+\n+The template folder is added to the search path of templates but with a lower \n+priority than the actual application's template folder. That way you can \n+easily override templates that a blueprint provides in the actual application. \n+This also means that you'll have to make sure that no blueprint template shares \n+the same relative path with any other blueprint or actual application template \n+else a n\n... (생략)\n```"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "That is much better, thank you for your input!"}]}
